WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.240
Detection engineering often begins by asking which behaviors matter most.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.920
Threat intelligence, adversary tactics, organizational priorities,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.465
and historical incidents can all inform where detection effort should be spent.

00:00:11.465 --> 00:00:15.000
The objective is to translate threat knowledge into observable signals.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.995
Instead of trying to monitor everything equally,

00:00:16.995 --> 00:00:20.850
teams identify behaviors that are relevant to their systems, data, users,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.000
and risk profile.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.385
A strong detection idea includes a hypothesis, required telemetry, expected behavior,

00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:28.435
investigation value, and assumptions.

00:00:28.435 --> 00:00:32.145
It should be clear why the behavior matters and what an analyst is expected to do when the

00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:33.000
signal appears.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.500
Risk-informed detection work helps teams make practical tradeoffs.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.000
Some detections may be high priority because they align to likely threats,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.910
protect critical services, or address lessons learned from recent operational experience.
